WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.660
Resource management is a security and reliability concern because applications depend on finite capacity.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:20.040
A C# service may look memory-safe at the language level and still fail users if it leaks handles, holds connections too long, stores unbounded data, or leaves cleanup paths unclear.

3
00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:27.100
Availability, correctness, data safety, and operational stability all depend on resources being used deliberately.

4
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.780
Resources are broader than memory.

5
00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:46.440
They include file handles, streams, sockets, database connections, HTTP connections, timers, locks, background tasks, queues, caches, thread pool capacity, external service capacity, and temporary storage.

6
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:54.380
Some are managed by the runtime, some are wrappers over operating system resources, and some exist in other systems that the application consumes.

7
00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.140
Leaks and long retention can degrade performance over time.

8
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:08.280
A missing disposal call, an unbounded queue, a cache with no size policy, or a background operation that never stops can quietly change system behavior.

9
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:21.260
The symptoms may appear as slow responses, blocked users, failed jobs, corrupted workflows, locked files, delayed cleanup, or sensitive temporary data left behind after a partial failure.

10
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:26.900
Unbounded resource use can create risk even when no traditional vulnerability is present.

11
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:36.760
A feature that accepts too much work, loads too much data, fans out without a limit, retries without control, or waits forever can make the system unreliable.

12
00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:44.380
Secure engineering treats limits, cleanup, and recovery behavior as part of the design, not as optional production tuning.

13
00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.180
A practical resource review starts with five questions.

14
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.880
Who owns the resource?

15
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.500
How long should it live?

16
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.280
What code releases it?

17
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.180
What limits protect the system if usage grows?

18
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.480
What signals show whether cleanup is working in production?

19
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:06.060
Answering those questions early makes the application easier to test, operate, and recover.
